{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-introduction",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# `rotation_matrix_from_vectors()`\n",
    "\n",
    "`nematics3d.rotation_matrix_from_vectors()` constructs a proper $3\\times3$ rotation matrix that maps one three-dimensional direction onto another. Input magnitudes are ignored: finite non-zero vectors are normalized internally. For nonparallel, non-antiparallel directions, it returns the unique minimal-angle rotation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-setup",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Setup\n",
    "\n",
    "**For readers who are only interested in the tutorial, this section can be safely skipped.** Run the following cell to import `NumPy` and `Nematics3D`; no setup detail is needed to understand the examples."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "rotation-matrix-imports",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import nematics3d as n3d"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-minimal",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Minimal example\n",
    "\n",
    "Rotate the positive $x$ direction onto the positive $y$ direction. The returned matrix $R$ satisfies $R\\mathbf{s}=\\mathbf{t}$, where $\\mathbf{s}$ and $\\mathbf{t}$ are the source and target unit vectors."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "rotation-matrix-minimal-code",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "source = np.array([1.0, 0.0, 0.0])\n",
    "target = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 0.0])\n",
    "\n",
    "R = n3d.rotation_matrix_from_vectors(source, target)\n",
    "print(R)\n",
    "print(\"rotated source:\", R @ source)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-properties",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Rotation-matrix properties\n",
    "\n",
    "The result is a proper orthogonal matrix: $R^T R=I$ and $\\det R=1$. Therefore it preserves lengths, angles, and handedness."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "rotation-matrix-properties-code",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"orthogonal:\", np.allclose(R.T @ R, np.eye(3)))\n",
    "print(\"determinant:\", np.linalg.det(R))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-inputs",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Inputs and output\n",
    "\n",
    "The public signature is:\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "rotation_matrix_from_vectors(source_vector, target_vector)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Both inputs must be finite non-zero vectors with shape `(3,)`. Their magnitudes are ignored and they are normalized internally, so `[2, 0, 0]` and `[1, 0, 0]` describe the same source direction. The function returns a `NumPy` array with shape `(3, 3)`. Zero, non-finite, or incorrectly shaped vectors are rejected."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-details",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Details\n",
    "\n",
    "For the generic case, let $c=\\mathbf{s}\\cdot\\mathbf{t}$ and $\\mathbf{u}=(\\mathbf{s}\\times\\mathbf{t})/\\|\\mathbf{s}\\times\\mathbf{t}\\|$. The implementation uses Rodrigues' rotation formula,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$R=I+\\sin\\theta\,[\\mathbf{u}]_\\times +(1-\\cos\\theta)[\\mathbf{u}]_\\times^2,$$\n",
    "\n",
    "with $\\cos\\theta=c$ and $\\sin\\theta=\\|\\mathbf{s}\\times\\mathbf{t}\\|$. This directly constructs the minimal rotation without solving a more general vector-alignment problem."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-parallel",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Parallel vectors\n",
    "\n",
    "If the source and target point in the same direction, the minimal rotation is the identity."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "rotation-matrix-parallel-code",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "v = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])\n",
    "v /= np.linalg.norm(v)\n",
    "print(n3d.rotation_matrix_from_vectors(v, v))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-antiparallel",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Antiparallel vectors\n",
    "\n",
    "For $\\mathbf{t}=-\\mathbf{s}$, the rotation angle is $\\pi$, but the rotation axis is not unique: every axis perpendicular to $\\mathbf{s}$ gives a valid minimal rotation. `Nematics3D` therefore uses a deterministic convention, choosing an axis from the Cartesian basis direction least aligned with the source. Do not attach physical meaning to that chosen axis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "rotation-matrix-antiparallel-code",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "source = np.array([1.0, 0.0, 0.0])\n",
    "R_opposite = n3d.rotation_matrix_from_vectors(source, -source)\n",
    "print(R_opposite)\n",
    "print(\"rotated source:\", R_opposite @ source)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-frame-use",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Rotating a reference frame\n",
    "\n",
    "A common use in `Nematics3D` is to construct transverse reference axes. Once $R$ maps one reference direction onto a desired direction, the same matrix can rotate the remaining basis vectors consistently."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "rotation-matrix-frame-code",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "normal = np.array([1.0, 1.0, 1.0])\n",
    "normal /= np.linalg.norm(normal)\n",
    "R_frame = n3d.rotation_matrix_from_vectors([0.0, 0.0, 1.0], normal)\n",
    "axis_x = R_frame @ np.array([1.0, 0.0, 0.0])\n",
    "axis_y = R_frame @ np.array([0.0, 1.0, 0.0])\n",
    "print(\"normal:\", R_frame @ np.array([0.0, 0.0, 1.0]))\n",
    "print(\"transverse dot products:\", axis_x @ normal, axis_y @ normal)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "rotation-matrix-limitations",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Requirements and limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "This function maps one direction onto another; it does not determine a complete target frame. A single vector leaves rotation about that vector unconstrained. The minimal-angle convention resolves that freedom in the generic case, while the exactly antiparallel case still requires the deterministic convention described above. If a specific transverse orientation matters physically, provide or construct that frame explicitly rather than inferring it from one vector."
   ]
  }
 ],
